replant
=======

Moves the hard-coded FHS paths in a source tree to the Zainium OS layout.

Zainium OS has no /usr, /etc or /var at the root. Software written for the
classic layout opens paths like /etc/passwd, so each package needs those
paths changed before it is built. Doing that by hand is slow and easy to get
wrong, and a blind sed can damage files.

replant reads a source tree and writes a patch, not changes in place, so a
person can read every change before it goes into a recipe.

Plan
----

  1. Set the build system's own options first (--prefix, --sysconfdir,
     --localstatedir, meson options), which fixes most paths with no patch.
  2. Find paths only inside string literals in C, C++, Rust, Python and
     shell, never in comments, docs or binary files.
  3. Match whole path segments only, so /etcd or a URL is left alone.
  4. Never touch /proc, /sys, /dev, /tmp, /run or /home.
  5. Write 0001-zainium-paths.patch and a report of what changed and what
     looked unsure.

Paths built at run time can not be found this way. sys_resolver still
covers those on the running system.

Usage
-----

    replant map                         the path map
    replant find <source-dir>           every path it would change, with file and line
    replant patch <source-dir> -o 0001-zainium-paths.patch
    replant verify <source-dir>         exits 1 if any hard-coded path is left

It reads C, C++, Go, Vala, JavaScript, Rust, Python, shell, Perl and Ruby,
and skips tests, docs, translations and vendored code.

Status
------

Early. Check every change in the patch before using it: a path inside a
message or a comment-like string is changed too.

